],["",32,-1,1],["",33,-1,1]],0,1044],[7084698,20395,117,2.361,1.866,[[28,39]],[["",34,-6,1],["",34,-3,1]],0,1045],[7111846,20395,30,0.737,0.556,[[28,40]],[],0,1046],[7101066,20396,237,4.806,3.833,[[47,0.552,8,131],[40,0.241,4,57],[49,0.207,1,49]],[["26b90ba3_fed147de",28,-1,1,"I see this pattern more and more, is there a reason? My understand is we build with Clang C++ Hardening and that `task_queues_[queue_priority]` will also perform OOB checks?"],["afbb341b_62b5693c",28,-1,1,"Many queues will have the same name? We're okay with that?"]],0,1047],[7121619,20397,3,0.12,0.081,[[44,26]],[],0,1048],[7121398,20398,64,2.212,1.739,[[46,0.694,4,44],[40,0.153,5,10],[48,0.153,2,10]],[["5d1fe426_1a17577f",35,-1,0,"The naming is a bit misleading considering we're not using TaskQueueImpl::InsertFence() - this isn't a fence in the TaskQueue sense."]],0,1049],[7125680,20400,38,1.341,1.058,[[53,0]],[],0,1050],[7127860,20402,41,1.341,1.058,[[28,0.5,41,20],[38,0.5,3,21]],[["f9c35941_1d99ee9c",36,-4,1,"```suggestion if (provider == nullptr || !provider->is_registry_ready()) { ```"],["fa50c3e9_3a723471",37,-4,1,"can you make this USER_VISIBLE? This is important for, for example, ChromeOS managed devices, where we need to get the policy for force-installed apps. (this might fix your issue too)"]],0,1051],[7125179,20402,2,0.078,0.051,[[1,65]],[],0,1052],[7147940,20404,347,1.79,1.419,[[12,0.506,30,175],[30,0.469,172,165],[26,0.017,119,5],[32,0.008,121,2]],[],0,1053],[7153439,20405,174,4.21,3.407,[[25,6]],[],0,1054],[7142025,20406,50,0.415,0.244,[[54,53]],[],0,1055],[7139599,20406,68,2.361,1.866,[[55,0.889,0,62],[28,0.069,42,4],[38,0.042,4,2]],[["4c9c17c2_43ecfb20",38,-3,0,"nit: `ASSERT_NE(nullptr, proxy)`"],["cbaaad63_063b898c",19,-3,1,"Would it make sense to have a version of PreferredAppUpdateWaiter that just takes a profile and an app id, if you have to duplicate this code a bunch?"],["de848a0e_aad23a5b",19,-3,0,"ditto"],["520b9702_39ceaa81",39,0,1,"If this is to be used inside of InteractiveBrowserTest/InteractiveAshTest, the run_loop_ should be constructed with the type `kNestableTasksAllowed`."]],0,1056],[7148066,20406,250,2.475,1.932,[[11,0.626,8,157],[12,0.191,31,48],[30,0.117,173,29],[35,0.066,3,16]],[["077fb53c_774cc2dd",40,0,1,"can you make this more specific? i.e. only allow the flat_hash_map.h header?"]],0,1057],[7147330,20406,312,4.911,3.884,[[56,0.877,25,275],[11,0.123,9,37]],[],0,1058],[7163775,20410,189,2,1.263,[[33,0.75,15,143],[57,0.25,0,46]],[],0,1059],[7167751,20411,313,1.79,1.419,[[11,0.945,10,299],[58,0.025,12,6],[12,0.018,32,5],[59,0.012,17,3]],[["765a55fd_99c9d94d",41,0,0,"Just to understand, are both cases actually happening, or is the observer list always created first now?"]],0,1060],[7200245,20416,5,0.157,0.113,[[0,113]],[],0,1061],[7121638,20417,568,9.607,7.821,[[40,0.87,6,498],[58,0.078,13,43],[46,0.045,5,24],[60,0.007,7,3]],[["52bcd955_c144565d",42,-21,1,"These could technically be different pointers, what matters is that the tasks they run satisfy the same SequenceChecker. Let's avoid ptr comparison as it would prevent TaskEnvironment from say…"],["983f6af5_3ae12add",43,-6,0,"Still not a fan of ptr equality checks. These could technically return a new scoped_refptr each time as long as the underlying queues are the same. Would a queue-equality helper (which uses…"],["fe3cb506_a21a673c",43,-6,1,"ThreadCheckerImpl would be most appropriate for SingleThreadTaskRunner"]],0,1062],[7205221,20420,191,3.856,3.092,[[36,33]],[["12f9e9ce_d35bbd3b",1,0,1,"speens => speeds ?"],["c7a95321_8f26cc77",16,0,0,"No longer trivial, would ideally move to .cc? (I actually don't see why other methods aren't in the .cc file as well though, perhaps it's best to keep that separate from this change)"]],0,1063],[7209835,20423,639,9.607,7.821,[[36,0.955,34,613],[37,0.042,71,25],[5,0.003,28,1]],[["2f2cd784_710412ba",16,0,1,"Let's document memory order semantics here as it took me some time to grasp: ``` // The current head Node for this bucket, potentially updated under `lock_` with // memory_order_release semantics…"],["332fa526_d146402e",17,0,0,"Redundant with \"estimated\""],["3a1fc506_32ed0446",16,0,1,"drive-by (reading existing code): The use of `bucket_mask_ = bucket_count - 1` requires `bucket_count` to be a power of 2, right? Let's document and assert that?"],["41309fe2_38d0aba9",44,0,0,"This can result in setting the same bit twice right? i.e. setting say 7 bits total instead of 8 in`bitmask` after 8 iterations. We're okay with that? Worth documenting on `BitsPerKey` ?"],["5ce9fc7f_f9d204b9",17,0,1,"drive-by: Looks like there's a bug on matching `load()`'s as there are no matching `std::memory_order_acquire` in this file. It is correct to release here (i.e. any reader which sees this new `head`…"],["8a777d2f_993b9e88",44,0,0,"Is this really `std::atomic<T>::is_lock_free` on 32-bit arch? Let's assert this here."],["9aa8bc4e_a607eaec",16,0,0,"Why is that? My understanding is we need 5 bits per index (`2^6 = 64` bit indices) and and size_t gives us 32 bits on 32-bit platforms. `32/5 = 6` indices available within a 32-bit size_t hash? Also…"],["9cb186a3_5d50ac44",16,0,1,"drive-by: I don't see why we need to overload const, the lookup seems const and non-const callers can call the const method."],["a8ac5107_ccc9f267",17,0,1,"// Rebuild the bloom filter as we go, without the removed key."],["ef584d93_89b5e4a0",44,0,1,"`static` not required/recommended for POD local consts"]],0,1064],[7216370,20424,10,0.122,0.075,[[1,66]],[],0,1065],[7224551,20425,4,0.107,0.074,[[1,67]],[],0,1066],[7227982,20427,52,1.22,0.938,[[61,4]],[],0,1067],[7228086,20429,48,1.233,0.942,[[5,29]],[],0,1068],[7228326,20430,25,0.518,0.377,[[56,0.567,26,15],[62,0.167,1,4],[20,0.167,29,4],[44,0.1,27,2]],[],0,1069],[7229005,20430,20,1.057,0.887,[[58,0.583,14,13],[45,0.25,6,4],[62,0.167,2,3]],[],0,1070],[7228085,20430,221,2.983,2.275,[[56,1.0,27,223]],[],0,1071],[7217451,20431,244,3.856,3.092,[[63,0.475,2,117],[0,0.341,114,86],[64,0.067,0,1